diagnosis_text:
I investigated the IT (192.168.1.0/24) segment and switch0 and found no network fault. Layer-2 on switch0 is healthy: the bridge br0 is UP with eth0 (to router0) and eth1 (to lan_it) both UP/LOWER_UP, and ethtool reports Link detected: yes on those ports. Router0’s interface for the IT VLAN is correctly configured (192.168.1.1/24 on eth0).

End-to-end testing from multiple IT hosts shows full reachability:
- it_hod (192.168.1.2) can ping router0 (192.168.1.1), office_pc (192.168.3.2), and an Internet host (128.168.0.5) with 0% loss.
- it_lab1–it_lab4 (192.168.1.3–.6) each have default via 192.168.1.1 and successfully ping router0, office_pc (192.168.3.2), the principal LAN (192.168.4.2), and Internet (128.168.0.5), all with 0% loss.
This confirms switching on lan_it, the uplink to router0, and inter-VLAN routing are operating normally.

Given these results, the issue described (“every 192.168.1.x machine can’t reach anything”) is not reproducing and does not align with current network health. A plausible explanation is a transient or application-layer problem (e.g., DNS resolution failure) that made hosts appear “offline” despite IP connectivity.

Root cause: No persistent infrastructure fault found; likely a transient client/DNS/application issue outside switch0/router0.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. If users still report issues, validate by pinging known IPs vs hostnames, check the DNS server/proxy on affected PCs, and collect traceroute/ping during the event to isolate any non-network causes. === END ===